    {
      "id": "ae8f1518bc72716e1871fc884d8b7b87da491c72",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1590449300,
          "type": null,
          "body": "This is related to the issue I addressed in !12198.\n\nCMake builds of libraries, by default, give the library @rpath/<library name> as the install path of the library.\n\nThat's what the stuff in the osx-app script was working around.\n\nHowever, it also, at least with Xcode 15 (which got \"helpfully\" installed on my Ventura machine while I wasn't watching), and Sonoma and at least newer versions of Ventura, causes programs built in the Wireshark build directory not to run.\n\n!12198 adds a `DO_CMAKE` variable, set to `\"cmake -DCMAKE_MACOSX_RPATH=OFF -DCMAKE_INSTALL_NAME_DIR=/usr/local/lib\"`, and runs CMake with `$DO_CMAKE` rather than just `cmake`, to force it to make the install path /usr/local/lib/<library name> rather than @rpath/<library name>.  It might even fix libbrotli.\n\nThis means that *if* you've either installed the libraries from scratch with a recent tools/macos-setup.sh, or have uninstalled the offending libraries and rebuilt them, there shouldn't be any Wireshark dependencies in /usr/local/lib with @rpath/<library name> paths, so that fixing might no longer be necessary.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-10-04T23:04:16.276Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-10-04T23:04:16.276Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 254930793,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
